---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CodeExample from "../../components/CodeExample.astro";
import PageIntro from "../../components/PageIntro.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../consts";
import lessonSource from "../../generated/examples/tutorial-standard-library.kwe?raw";

const source = lessonSource.trimEnd();
const output = `eziokwu
ụgha
eziokwu
eziokwu
6
Ndewo, Kwechiri
eziokwu
Kwechiri
ụbọchị
ỌMA
KweKwe`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Lesson 8: Standard Library Strings"
  description="Learn the nine Kwechiri string functions for exact matching, measurement, and transformation."
>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="Tutorial 08"
    title="Standard Library Strings"
    lead="Import the official kwechiri:eriri module, inspect and transform strings, count user-perceived characters, and join text exactly."
    meta="Beginner · Complete Lesson 7 first"
  />

  <div class="shell content-layout">
    <nav class="docs-nav" aria-label="Tutorial navigation">
      <a href="/tutorials/modules/">← Lesson 7: Modules</a>
      <a href="/tutorials/standard-library-strings/" aria-current="page">
        Lesson 8: Standard Library strings
      </a>
    </nav>
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>The program</h2>
      <p>Open <code>examples/tutorials/standard-library.kwe</code>.</p>
      <CodeExample
        label="examples/tutorials/standard-library.kwe"
        code={source}
        output={output}
      />

      <h2>Run it</h2>
      <p>From the repository root, run the entry file:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>node dist/cli/index.js examples/tutorials/standard-library.kwe</code></pre>

      <h2>Importing a standard module</h2>
      <p>
        Standard Library modules use the same explicit import form as filesystem
        modules, but their names begin with the reserved
        <code>kwechiri:</code> namespace:
      </p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>webata &#123; nwereEriri, maliteNa, kwụsịNa, ogologoEriri, jikọọEriri, wepuOghere, okwuNta, okwuNnukwu, dochieEriri &#125; site na "kwechiri:eriri"</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>kwechiri:eriri</code> is provided by the compiler. It is not a
        package, remote URL, or hidden <code>.kwe</code> file. Only the imported
        names become available, and the import must come before other
        statements.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Function</th><th>Signature</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>nwereEriri</code></td><td><code>(string, string) -&gt; boolean</code></td><td>Contains</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>maliteNa</code></td><td><code>(string, string) -&gt; boolean</code></td><td>Starts with</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>kwụsịNa</code></td><td><code>(string, string) -&gt; boolean</code></td><td>Ends with</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ogologoEriri</code></td><td><code>(string) -&gt; number</code></td><td>Grapheme length</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>jikọọEriri</code></td><td><code>(string, string) -&gt; string</code></td><td>Exact concatenation</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>wepuOghere</code></td><td><code>(string) -&gt; string</code></td><td>Trim surrounding whitespace</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>okwuNta</code></td><td><code>(string) -&gt; string</code></td><td>Unicode lowercase</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>okwuNnukwu</code></td><td><code>(string) -&gt; string</code></td><td>Unicode uppercase</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>dochieEriri</code></td><td><code>(string, string, string) -&gt; string</code></td><td>Literal replace all</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Exact string checks</h2>
      <p>
        <code>nwereEriri</code>, <code>maliteNa</code>, and
        <code>kwụsịNa</code> compare exact string sequences. Matching is
        case-sensitive:
      </p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>nwereEriri("Kwechiri", "chiri") # eziokwu
nwereEriri("Kwechiri", "CHIRI") # ụgha</code></pre>
      <p>
        An empty search string is contained in every string and is both a
        prefix and a suffix of every string. That is why
        <code>maliteNa(aha, "")</code> prints <code>eziokwu</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Counting characters</h2>
      <p>
        <code>ogologoEriri</code> counts extended grapheme clusters: characters
        as a reader perceives them, rather than raw UTF-16 storage units.
      </p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>ogologoEriri("ụbọchị") # 6</code></pre>
      <p>
        An Igbo letter written with combining marks still counts as one
        character. An empty string has length <code>0</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Joining strings</h2>
      <p>
        <code>jikọọEriri</code> joins two raw strings without inserting spaces
        or separators and without converting other value types:
      </p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>jikọọEriri("Ndewo, ", "Kwechiri") # Ndewo, Kwechiri</code></pre>
      <p>
        The space is part of the first argument. String contents are not
        normalized, so visually similar NFC and NFD sequences remain different
        raw strings.
      </p>

      <h2>Transforming strings</h2>
      <p>
        <code>wepuOghere</code> removes whitespace only at the beginning and
        end, preserving interior whitespace:
      </p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>wepuOghere("  Kwechiri  ") # Kwechiri
wepuOghere("Kwe chiri")     # Kwe chiri</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>okwuNta</code> and <code>okwuNnukwu</code> use locale-independent
        Unicode case mapping without normalization:
      </p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>okwuNta("ỤBỌCHỊ")    # ụbọchị
okwuNnukwu("ọma")     # ỌMA</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>dochieEriri</code> replaces every exact, non-overlapping literal
        occurrence. Matching is case-sensitive and is not a regular expression:
      </p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>dochieEriri("a-b-a", "-", "+") # a+b+a</code></pre>

      <h2>Your turn</h2>
      <p>Add these lines and predict the output:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>bipụta(nwereEriri(aha, ""))
bipụta(ogologoEriri("Igbo"))
bipụta(jikọọEriri("Kwe", "chiri"))</code></pre>
      <p>
        Expected output: <code>eziokwu</code>, <code>4</code>, and
        <code>Kwechiri</code>.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout--gold">
        <strong>Common problems</strong>
        Import the module and name every function you use. Keep the import
        first, provide the exact argument count and string types, and remember
        that predicates are case-sensitive. Length counts user-perceived
        characters, concatenation adds no separator, and replacement is literal.
      </div>

      <p>
        The original five names are frozen for Kwechiri 1.0; Batch 11C adds the
        four transformation functions.
      </p>

      <h2>Normative references</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/standard-library/README.md`}>Standard Library</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/standard-library/strings.md`}>String functions</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/language-spec/modules.md`}>Standard modules</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/design-decisions/ADR-022-standard-library-signatures-and-errors.md`}>Standard Library signatures and errors</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/design-decisions/ADR-023-string-basics.md`}>String functions decision</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/language-spec/errors.md`}>Current language errors</a></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Next</h2>
      <p>
        Continue to <a href="/tutorials/multiline-strings/">Lesson 9: Multiline
        strings</a>, then explore the <a href="/examples/">verified examples</a>
        and the <a href="/docs/language/">language overview</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
